{"abstract":"Self-organized partitioning into subgroups is often a desirable skill of a swarm system, as seen in examples like honeybee swarms dividing into task forces or worker robots spreading across different target regions with various demands. Each agent in the swarm selects a target region based on its perception of the world’s current state, i.e. worker count and work demand in each region. It’s intriguing to assume that the better agents perceive the world, the more efficiently the swarm performs. In this study, we compare a swarm with flawless perception of the world’s state, i.e. access to global information, to one where robots estimate the state through local interactions. Unexpectedly, our results reveal that the swarm relying on local estimates outperforms the one with perfect perception. Further exploration into information access timing suggests that a slower timescale of access might play a key role in the swarm’s success.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Zahadat, Payam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-730194"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Information","Resource distribution","Self organization","Swarm robotics","Timescales"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"local estimation vs global information the benefits of slower timescales","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102343507/LocalvsGlobal.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-730194","identifier":[{"id":"85181540011","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ca23ffbc-ea24-4e2b-b250-6dc53bfe9404"],"keyword":["Information","Resource distribution","Self-organization","Swarm robotics","Timescales"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-02DB5783AB9BA08799E69C0A88410DE8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102343507/LocalvsGlobal.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/acsos-c58168.2023.00030","eisbn":["IEEE Catalog Number: CFP23X51ART"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"4th IEEE International Conference on Autonomic Computing and Self-Organizing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85181540011"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ca23ffbc-ea24-4e2b-b250-6dc53bfe9404"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Local Estimation vs Global Information: the Benefits of Slower Timescales","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-730194","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"In many self-organising systems the ability to extract necessary resources from the external environment is essential for growth and survival.In many self-organising systems the ability to extract necessary resources from the external environment is essential for growth and survival. E.g., extracting sunlight and nutrients in organic plants, monetary income in business organisations and mobile robots in intelligent swarms. When operating within competitive, changing environments, such systems must distribute their assets wisely, to improve and adapt their ability to extract available resources. As the system size increases, the asset-distribution process often gets organised around a multi-scale control topology. This topology may be static (fixed) or dynamic (enabling growth and structural adaptation) depending on the system's constraints and adaptive mechanisms. In this paper we expand on a plant-inspired asset-distribution model and study the impact that the topology of the multi-scale control process has upon the system's ability to self-adapt asset distribution when resource availability changes within the environment. Results show how different topological characteristics and different competition levels between system branches impact overall system profitability, adaptation delays and disturbances when environmental changes occur. These findings provide a basis for system designers to select the most suitable topology and configuration for their particular application and execution environment.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Zahadat, Payam","Diaconscu, Ada"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383802"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Télécom Paris"],"keyword":["multi scale control topology","self adaptive asset distribution"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the impact of multi scale control topology on asset distribution in dynamic environments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACSOSC56246.2022.00023","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383802","identifier":[{"id":"85143070883","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ae6b6d19-df70-4f94-a457-bd05bfbe7653"],"keyword":["multi-scale control topology","self-adaptive asset distribution"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C3B345D804FC273474CF41D6A40F2A8C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90739398/Zahadat2022ACSOS.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACSOSC56246.2022.00023","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"télécom paris","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Télécom Paris","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ada","key":"diaconscupau","last":"Diaconscu","name":"Diaconscu, Ada","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/acsosc56246.2022.00023","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"3rd IEEE International Conference on Autonomic Computing and Self-Organizing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85143070883"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ae6b6d19-df70-4f94-a457-bd05bfbe7653"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The impact of multi-scale control topology on asset distribution in dynamic environments","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383802","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In many self-organising systems the ability to extract necessary resources from the external environment is essential to the system's growth and survival. Examples include the extraction of sunlight and nutrients in organic plants, of monetary income in business organisations and of mobile robots in swarm intelligence actions. When operating within competitive, ever-changing environments, such systems must distribute their internal assets wisely so as to improve and adapt their ability to extract available resources. As the system size increases, the asset-distribution process often gets organised around a multi-scale control topology. This topology may be static (fixed) or dynamic (enabling growth and structural adaptation) depending on the system's internal constraints and adaptive mechanisms. In this paper, we expand on a plant-inspired asset-distribution model and introduce a more general multi-scale model applicable across a wider range of natural and artificial system domains. We study the impact that the topology of the multi-scale control process has upon the system's ability to self-adapt asset distribution when resource availability changes within the environment. Results show how different topological characteristics and different competition levels between system branches impact overall system profitability, adaptation delays and disturbances when environmental changes occur. These findings provide a basis for system designers to select the most suitable topology and configuration for their particular application and execution environment.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Zahadat, Payam","Diaconscu, Ada"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383083"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Télécom Paris"],"keyword":["Resource Extraction","environmental competition","multi scale control","self organising systems","topology adaptation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multi scale asset distribution model for dynamic environments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90739516/Zahadat2022arxiv.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-383083","ids":["itu-7b818190-e5fd-4f16-afcc-8d7078ea86c5"],"keyword":["environmental competition","multi-scale control","resource extraction","self-organising systems","topology adaptation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-14CBB98A2DA746E512F4A9D3CC580B50"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90739516/Zahadat2022arxiv.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"télécom paris","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Télécom Paris","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ada","key":"diaconscupau","last":"Diaconscu","name":"Diaconscu, Ada","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7b818190-e5fd-4f16-afcc-8d7078ea86c5"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multi-Scale Asset Distribution Model for Dynamic Environments","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383083","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In many complex systems, from robot and insect swarms to human social systems, agents take decisions collectively, using information retrieved from the environment and from each other. This information is usually correlated to some extent – e.g., voters reading the same media outlets, animals receiving the same cues from their environment, or people listening to the same opinion leaders. Taking inspiration from human social systems, we consider the case of collective decision-making between two choices, one being the correct one. We break down the problem of collective decision making in correlated environments into two components: (i) how likely different configurations of information environments are to show the correct option and (ii) how likely different configurations of collectives are to detect the majority shown by the environment. An agent-based model is presented, where agents scan an information environment, composed of correlated and uncorrelated sources, and form individual opinions based on the information perceived. Once individual opinions are formed through a majority function, agents take a majority vote to determine their chosen option. Preliminary results show how different population parameters lead to different decision accuracy in similar information environments, and how the two steps of opinion formation and collective vote can skew the collective’s perception of the environment positively or negatively. Future work will expand these results by allowing agents to form local groups before taking decisions collectively.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Di Felice, Louisa Jane","Zahadat, Payam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384770"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agent based modeling","Collective decision making","Correlated Environments","Information Perception","Opinion formation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"an agent based model of collective decision making in correlated environments","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90740871/DiFelice_Zahadat_ABMHuB22.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384770","ids":["itu-edbc9d25-eb7c-4407-8b6d-b6a41d96fdac"],"keyword":["Agent-Based Modeling","Collective Decision-Making","Correlated Environments","Information Perception","Opinion Formation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E8B2DA225884BD51F285FA7A72EF36AB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/90740871/DiFelice_Zahadat_ABMHuB22.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Robotics, Evolution, and Art Lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Louisa Jane","key":"difelicepau","last":"Di Felice","name":"Di Felice, Louisa Jane","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-edbc9d25-eb7c-4407-8b6d-b6a41d96fdac"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"An Agent-Based Model of Collective Decision-making in Correlated Environments","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384770","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"In evolutionary robotics, an encoding of the control software that maps sensor data (input) to motor control values (output) is shaped by stochastic optimization methods to complete a predefined task. This approach is assumed to be beneficial compared to standard methods of controller design in those cases where no a priori model is available that could help to optimize performance. For robots that have to operate in unpredictable environments as well, an evolutionary robotics approach is favorable. We present here a simple-to-implement, but hard-to-pass benchmark to allow for quantifying the “evolvability” of such evolving robot control software towards increasing behavioral complexity. We demonstrate that such a model-free approach is not a free lunch, as already simple tasks can be unsolvable barriers for fully open-ended uninformed evolutionary computation techniques. We propose the “Wankelmut” task as an objective for an evolutionary approach that starts from scratch without pre-shaped controller software or any other informed approach that would force the behavior to be evolved in a desired way. Our main claim is that “Wankelmut” represents the simplest set of problems that makes plain-vanilla evolutionary computation fail. We demonstrate this by a series of simple standard evolutionary approaches using different fitness functions and standard artificial neural networks, as well as continuous-time recurrent neural networks. All our tested approaches failed. From our observations, we conclude that other evolutionary approaches will also fail if they do not per se favor or enforce the modularity of the evolved structures and if they do not freeze or protect already evolved functionalities from being destroyed again in the later evolutionary process. However, such a protection would require a priori knowledge of the solution of the task and contradict the “no a priori model” approach that is often claimed in evolutionary computation. Thus, we propose a hard-to-pass benchmark in order to make a strong statement for self-complexifying and generative approaches in evolutionary computation in general and in evolutionary robotics specifically. We anticipate that defining such a benchmark by seeking the simplest task that causes the evolutionary process to fail can be a valuable benchmark for promoting future development in the fields of artificial intelligence, evolutionary robotics, and artificial life.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Schmickl, Thomas","Zahadat, Payam","Hamann, Heiko"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383214"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Agent based model","Artificial neural networks","CTRNN","Complexity","Evolutionary computation","Stochastic optimization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"wankelmut a simple benchmark for the evolvability of behavioral complexity","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app11051994","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383214","identifier":[{"id":"85102179289","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-844092c6-4df6-4467-9113-b58f7d2875d9"],"keyword":["CTRNN","agent-based model","artificial neural networks","complexity","evolutionary computation","stochastic optimization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-53A1FE8F58D3DA88410E67E5BBCEB002"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/app11051994","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"schmicklpau","last":"Schmickl","name":"Schmickl, Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Heiko","key":"hamannpau","last":"Hamann","name":"Hamann, Heiko","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/app11051994","issn":["20763417"],"issue":"5","journal":"Applied Sciences","publisher":"Balkan Society of Geometers","status":"p","title":"Applied Sciences","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85102179289"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-844092c6-4df6-4467-9113-b58f7d2875d9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Wankelmut: A Simple Benchmark for the Evolvability of Behavioral Complexity","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383214","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Designing controllers for agents of a collective system is challenging. The challenge lies in the nonlinearity between the behavior of the individuals and the emerging patterns in the collective. A branch of ongoing research in collective systems, e.g., in swarm robotics, concerns with discovering mechanisms that lead to specific collective behaviors indirectly, i.e., the effects of incentives in the emergence of interesting collective patterns. Various intrinsic motivations have been suggested as the drivers of pattern development in natural and artificial collective systems. An example is the development of pathways to provide easier access to currents that flow through a system. Another example is the emergence of collective motion as a result of intrinsic motivation for maximization of potential future states. By getting inspiration from the principle of free energy minimization in biological systems, predictability of the future states have also been used as an intrinsic motivation, resulting in a number of collective formations. It demonstrated the tendency of agents to locate themselves in the positions that are less prone to changes in their surroundings. The current paper presents early investigations of the agents’ incentive for avoiding the surrounding environment by locating themselves between their peers, i.e., an incentive for self-protection. The swarm behavior resulted from this intrinsic motivation leads to formation of aggregates with high mobility of agents within them. The behavior is loosely similar to the huddling behavior of emperor penguins, where the birds self-organize to take turn in locating themselves inside the crowd to stay protected from the wind. The behavior appears to show a relatively low sensitivity to the swarm density.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Zahadat, Payam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383117"],"event":{"acronym":"ECAL","title":"European Conference on Artificial Life"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Collective Systems","Free Energy Minimization","Intrinsic motivation","Self protection Mechanism","Swarm robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"incentive for self protection in a collective system a swarm robotics case study","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00427","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383117","ids":["itu-7e439738-f368-48b6-8764-0984bb19447f"],"keyword":["- Collective Systems","- Free Energy Minimization","- Intrinsic Motivation","- Self-protection Mechanism","- Swarm Robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-890E4F3A92BD1EDECA104D2EC79C2E02"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/isal_a_00427","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86201466/ZahadatALIFE2021.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1162/isal_a_00427","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","title":"ALIFE 2021: The 2021 Conference on Artificial Life"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7e439738-f368-48b6-8764-0984bb19447f"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Incentive for self-protection in a collective system: a swarm robotics case study","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383117","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"We develop here a novel hypothesis that may generate a general research framework of how autonomous robots may act as a future contingency to counteract the ongoing ecological mass extinction process. We showcase several research projects that have undertaken first steps to generate the required prerequisites for such a technology-based conservation biology approach. Our main idea is to stabilise and support broken ecosystems by introducing artificial members, robots, that are able to blend into the ecosystem’s regulatory feedback loops and can modulate natural organisms’ local densities through participation in those feedback loops. These robots are able to inject information that can be gathered using technology and to help the system in processing available information with technology. In order to understand the key principles of how these robots are capable of modulating the behaviour of large populations of living organisms based on interacting with just a few individuals, we develop novel mathematical models that focus on important behavioural feedback loops. These loops produce relevant group-level effects, allowing for robotic modulation of collective decision making in social organisms. A general understanding of such systems through mathematical models is necessary for designing future organism-interacting robots in an informed and structured way, which maximises the desired output from a minimum of intervention. Such models also help to unveil the commonalities and specificities of the individual implementations and allow predicting the outcomes of microscopic behavioural mechanisms on the ultimate macroscopic-level effects. We found that very similar models of interaction can be successfully used in multiple very different organism groups and behaviour types (honeybee aggregation, fish shoaling, and plant growth). Here we also report experimental data from biohybrid systems of robots and living organisms. Our mathematical models serve as building blocks for a deep understanding of these biohybrid systems. Only if the effects of autonomous robots onto the environment can be sufficiently well predicted can such robotic systems leave the safe space of the lab and can be applied in the wild to be able to unfold their ecosystem-stabilising potential.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Schmickl, Thomas","Szopek, Martina","Mondada, Francesco","Mills, Rob","Stefanec, Martin","Hofstadler, Daniel","Lazic, Dajana","Barmak, Rafael","Bonnet, Frank","Zahadat, Payam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382287"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Robot animal interaction","ecosystem collapse","organismic augmentation","robot organism interaction biohybrid systems biomimicry"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"social integrating robots suggest mitigation strategies for ecosystem decay","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2021.612605","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382287","identifier":[{"id":"85107336029","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-44c885a3-6d2e-4b1d-b5a8-fd3e86cee83b"],"keyword":["ecosystem collapse","organismic augmentation","robot–animal interaction","robot–organism interaction, biohybrid systems, biomimicry"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7B2CAA4C9B915379744C374B64AECF36"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86233023/SocialIntegratingRobots.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2021.612605","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"schmicklpau","last":"Schmickl","name":"Schmickl, Thomas","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Martina","key":"szopekpau","last":"Szopek","name":"Szopek, Martina","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Francesco","key":"mondadapau","last":"Mondada","name":"Mondada, Francesco","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rob","key":"millspau","last":"Mills","name":"Mills, Rob","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin","key":"stefanecpau","last":"Stefanec","name":"Stefanec, Martin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"hofstadlerpau","last":"Hofstadler","name":"Hofstadler, Daniel","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Dajana","key":"lazicpau","last":"Lazic","name":"Lazic, Dajana","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Rafael","key":"barmakpau","last":"Barmak","name":"Barmak, Rafael","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Frank","key":"bonnetpau","last":"Bonnet","name":"Bonnet, Frank","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fbioe.2021.612605","issn":["22964185"],"journal":"Frontiers in Bioengineering and Biotechnology","publisher":"Frontiers","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Bioengineering and Biotechnology"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85107336029"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-44c885a3-6d2e-4b1d-b5a8-fd3e86cee83b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Social Integrating Robots Suggest Mitigation Strategies for Ecosystem Decay","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382287","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Communication networks are prevalent in both natural and artificial systems, enabling information and resource exchanges amongst system parts. In most systems, the network topology influences system performance, which, in turn, reshapes the network topology; hence creating one or several feedback cycles. Understanding how such system growth and restructuring processes function becomes critical in today's increasingly connected world. This paper describes a generic model of feedback-based growth and adaptation for systems with tree-like control topologies. Inspired by plant vascular morphogenesis, the model is transferred here for studying the development and adaptation behaviour of business organisations, operating in fluctuating economic environments. Experimental results show the impact that various degrees of internal competition have on overall business growth, productivity and reactivity to changing business landscapes. The preliminary findings presented seem to fit existing economic studies on related topics. The proposed model offers a solid basis for studying morphogenetic processes and associated performance indicators, applied to tree-shaped business organisations, and transferable to further domains.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Zahadat, Payam","Diaconscu, Ada"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384900"],"event":{"acronym":"ECAL","title":"European Conference on Artificial Life"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Télécom Paris"],"keyword":["Business organisations","Communication networks","Feedback cycles","Network topology","Vascular morphogenesis"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reactive or stable a plant inspired approach for organisation morphogenesis","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551784/A_Plant_s_approach_for_structuring_an_organization_ALife2020.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384900","identifier":[{"id":"86000196701","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f676610d-a3c6-48a0-a1ec-f2fc27d64af9"],"keyword":["Business organisations","Communication networks","Feedback cycles","Network topology","Vascular morphogenesis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-5DCD072850EEFC4D3E95F843D210151D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85551784/A_Plant_s_approach_for_structuring_an_organization_ALife2020.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"fr","key":"télécom paris","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Télécom Paris","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ada","key":"diaconscupau","last":"Diaconscu","name":"Diaconscu, Ada","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1162/isal_a_00244","pages":"614-622","publisher":"MIT Press","status":"p","subtitle":"ALIFE 2020","title":"Artificial Life Conference Proceedings"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-86000196701"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f676610d-a3c6-48a0-a1ec-f2fc27d64af9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Reactive or Stable: A Plant-inspired Approach for Organisation Morphogenesis","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384900","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Distribution of resources can be a collective decision making process in both natural and artificial systems. The division of a colony into subtasks, the ant trails exploring the environment and connecting the nest to the food sources, and the growth and spread of branches in a tree to optimize the access to light, are different examples of collective distribution processes. In this paper, collective decision on distribution of resource is investigated via an algorithm inspired by plants morphogenesis. The algorithm, called Vascular Morphogenesis Controller (VMC), acts based on the competition of branches in a plant for limited shared resources - water, minerals, etc. The VMC algorithm decides on the changes in the shape of an artificial structure. The structure is a collective entity consisting of several nodes. The nodes act as agents running the algorithm and deciding collectively on the distribution of a shared resource. A change in the distribution of the resource may lead to addition or deletion of nodes and change the morphology of the structure. The system is dynamic and adaptive to variations in the environmental conditions (e.g. light). In this paper, the effects of different parameterizations of the algorithm on the morphology aspects of the collective structure (e.g. asymmetry and dynamicity) are studied analytically and numerically. A set of experiments are presented with the VMC embodied in a physical structure. The structure is made out of modules that hold VMC nodes and can be manually attached or detached following the suggestions of the algorithm. The experiments are performed with various parameters and environmental conditions (e.g. different lighting). The results support the findings of the theoretical study. The paper provides a deeper understanding of the functionalities of VMC and a basis for parameter selection according to desired behaviors. It also indicates similarities in some of the properties of this system and other collective systems suggesting potential benefits of viewing various types of collective systems from the perspective of resource distribution.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Zahadat, Payam","Hofstadler, Daniel Nicolas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384496"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Graz"],"keyword":["Collective decision making","Plant mechanisms","Resource distribution","Swarm intelligence","Transport networks","Vascular morphogenesis controller"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"toward a theory of collective resource distribution a study of a dynamic morphogenesis controller","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11721-019-00174-x","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384496","identifier":[{"id":"85072029145","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-db9e7d8f-ac8b-4544-870b-caafdd0c310c"],"keyword":["Collective decision making","Plant mechanisms","Resource distribution","Swarm intelligence","Transport networks","Vascular morphogenesis controller"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-FB5311AA87DFD95AF20C3E0C779AF335"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85683451/Zahadat_Hofstadler2019_Article_TowardATheoryOfCollectiveResou.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11721-019-00174-x","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"at","key":"university of graz","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Graz","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel Nicolas","key":"hofstadlerpau","last":"Hofstadler","name":"Hofstadler, Daniel Nicolas","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s11721-019-00174-x","issn":["19353812","19353820"],"issn_org":["19353812"],"journal":"Swarm Intelligence","pages":"347-380","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Swarm Intelligence","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85072029145"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-db9e7d8f-ac8b-4544-870b-caafdd0c310c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Toward a Theory of Collective Resource Distribution: A Study of a Dynamic Morphogenesis Controller","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384496","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Self-assembly in biological systems is an inspiration for engineered large-scale multi-modular systems with desirable characteristics, such as robustness, scalability, and adaptivity. Previous works have shown that simple mobile robots can be used to emulate and study self-assembly behaviors. However, many of these studies were restricted to rather static and inflexible aggregations in predefined shapes, and were limited in adaptivity compared to that observed in nature. We propose a photomorphogenesis approach for robots using our vascular morphogenesis model---a light-stimuli directed method for multi-robot self-assembly inspired by the tissue growth of trees. Robots in the role of `leaves' collect a virtual resource that is proportional to a real, sensed environmental feature. This resource is then shared throughout the whole robot aggregate and determines where it grows or shrinks as a reaction to the dynamic environment. In our approach the robots use supplemental bioinspired models to collectively select a seed robot to decide who starts to self-assemble (and where), or to assemble static aggregations. The robots then use our vascular morphogenesis model to aggregate in a directed way preferring bright areas, hence resembling natural phototropism (growth towards light). In this assembly, they are adaptive and able to react to a dynamic environment by collectively and autonomously rearranging the aggregate, discarding outdated parts and growing new ones. In representative experiments, the self-assembling robots collectively make rational decisions on where to grow. Cutting off parts of the aggregate triggers a self-organizing repair process in the robots, and the parts regrow. All these capabilities of adaptivity, collective decision-making, and self-repair in our robot self-assembly originate directly from self-organized behavior of the vascular morphogenesis model. Our approach opens up opportunities for self-assembly with reconfiguration on short time-scales with high adaptivity of dynamic forms and structures.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Divband Soorati, Mohammad","Heinrich, Mary Katherine","Ghofrani, Javad","Zahadat, Payam","Hamann, Heiko"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384950"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Dresden University of Applied Sciences","University of Lübeck"],"keyword":["Artificial Growth","Morphogenesis","Self assembly","Self repair","Swarm robotics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"photomorphogenesis for robot self assembly adaptivity collective decision making and self repair","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.2538670","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384950","identifier":[{"id":"85069775189","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-fa348803-0f37-44a8-8164-97cee7ea02d7"],"keyword":["artificial growth","morphogenesis","self-assembly","self-repair","swarm robotics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-4-D5E73742A2EB50F0387FEF63C3938EB7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.2538670","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84833944/Divband_Soorati_2019_Bioinspir._Biomim._14_056006.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"de","key":"university of lübeck","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lübeck","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"dresden university of applied sciences","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Dresden University of Applied Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mohammad","key":"divbandsooratipau","last":"Divband Soorati","name":"Divband Soorati, Mohammad","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mary Katherine","key":"heinrichpau","last":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Mary Katherine","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Javad","key":"ghofranipau","last":"Ghofrani","name":"Ghofrani, Javad","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Payam","key":"zahadatpau","last":"Zahadat","locid":"8785f18a-1bf3-4c63-8d8b-d3e5d516ca40","name":"Zahadat, Payam","orcid":"0000-0002-4521-7428","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Heiko","key":"hamannpau","last":"Hamann","name":"Hamann, Heiko","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5281/zenodo.2538670","issn":["17483182","17483190"],"issn_org":["17483182"],"journal":"Bioinspiration & Biomimetics","publisher":"IOP Publishing","status":"p","title":"Bioinspiration & Biomimetics"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85069775189"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fa348803-0f37-44a8-8164-97cee7ea02d7"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Photomorphogenesis for Robot Self-assembly: Adaptivity, Collective Decision-making, and Self-repair","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384950","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Biohybrid robotics takes an engineering approach to the expansion and exploitation of biological behaviors for application to automated tasks. Here we identify the construction of living buildings and infrastructure as a high-potential application domain for biohybrid robotics, and review technological advances relevant to its future development. Construction, civil infrastructure maintenance, and building occupancy in the last decades have comprised a major portion of economic production, energy consumption, and carbon emissions. Integrating biological organisms into automated construction tasks and permanent building components therefore has high potential for impact. Live materials can provide several advantages over standard synthetic construction materials, including self-repair of damage, increase rather than degradation of structural performance over time, resilience to corrosive environments, support of biodiversity, and mitigation of urban heat islands. Here we review relevant technologies, which are currently disparate. They span robotics, self-organizing systems, artificial life, construction automation, structural engineering, architecture, bioengineering, biomaterials, and molecular and cellular biology. In these disciplines, developments relevant to biohybrid construction and living buildings are in early stages, and typically are not exchanged between disciplines. We therefore consider this review useful to the future development of biohybrid engineering for this highly interdisciplinary application.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Heinrich, Mary Katherine","von Mammen, Sebastian","Hofstadler, Daniel Nicolas","Wahby, Mostafa","Zahadat, Payam","Skrzypczak, Tomasz","Divband Soorati, Mohammad","Krela, Rafal","Kwiatkowski, Wojciech","Schmickl, Thomas","Ayres, Phil","Stoy, Kasper","Hamann, Heiko"],"cuid":["S02-091.84-666042"],"filters":{"added":"2023-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Germany","Poland"],"glorg":["Adam Mickiewicz University","Adam Mickiewicz University in Poznań","All Global Affiliations","Julius-Maximilians-Universität Würzburg","University of Graz","University of Lübeck","University of Würzburg"],"keyword":["Bio hybrid","Biobot","Construction","Hybrid","Robotics","Self organisation","Self organization"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-ART","DI-U"],"level3":{"ART":["DI-ART-ALL","DI-ART-RDKA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"constructing living buildings a review of relevant technologies for a novel application of biohybrid robotics","src":["arkc","itu"],"src-code":["DI-ART-RDKA","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2019.0238","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-091.84-666042","identifier":[{"id":"85070269359","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["arkc-2f61f70f-9974-4ddd-86c2-49506a657f3f","itu-acd3a16c-1836-4ff8-8447-34f1f3375642"],"keyword":["Bio-hybrid","Biobot","Construction","Hybrid","No","Robotics","Self-organisation","self-organization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-94FD87EB246EF2C97AF6D027879A90ED"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/84759307/rsif.2019.0238.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/334062542","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2019.0238","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"royal danish academy architecture design conservation","l2":"DI-ART","l2n":"Art